  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nSfc high pressure will maintain its presence across the \nDakotas/Minnesota border region tonight into Thursday. An upper \nlevel disturbance working through northwest flow aloft across \nsouthwest SD brought rain showers to parts of south central SD today \nwill shift south of the region tonight. Any leftover showers in that \npart of the state should be short lived and gone by late \nafternoon/early evening. Temperatures overnight will maintain a near \nnormal to slightly below normal range.\n\nThe center of the high will shift southeast of the region on \nThursday. This will promote a more pronounced southerly flow across \nour forecast area. An upper level disturbance tracking across \nwestern SD into western NE will bring another round of showers and \nmaybe a thunderstorm or two to parts of south central SD on \nThursday. There remains about a 30-60 percent chance for seeing a \ntenth of an inch or more of rainfall from Stanley and Hughes \nCounties east to southern Hand and points south and west. \nTemperatures will be coolest on those areas where clouds and \nrainfall hang tough most of the day, while the rest of the area sees \nnear normal temperatures.\n\nHeat will start to build into the region on Friday as upper ridging \ndevelops across the Northern Plains. 850mb thermal progs indicate \n+19C to +23C in our eastern zones while our western zones should see \n+25C to +30C Friday afternoon. A cold front is progged to sag south \ninto northern SD Friday night into early Saturday. The associated \nlow pressure system and upper level support will remain across \neastern ND and northwest MN where severe convection is expected. SPC \nhas highlighted those areas back into central ND for a Marginal Risk \n(level 1 of 5) for severe weather. This risk does touch into a small \nportion of north central SD. Some of the hi-res, CAMs and \ndeterministic guidance does show a storm or two could skirt across \nnorthern SD Friday night. After that system clears the area, the \nupper ridge will continue to build overhead through the weekend. \n850mb temps are progged to range from the upper 20s Celsius to lower \n30s Celsius across our eastern zones to the low to mid 30s Celsius \nacross our western zones by Sunday into Monday. Widespread \ntemperatures in the 90s in most locations to low 100s across central \nSD are expected Saturday through early next week. Another fropa \nwill be possible late Sat/early Sun which may touch off a storm or \ntwo across ND into parts of SD. However, a stronger cap is progged to \nbuild in with 700mb temps reaching +14C to +18C across our forecast \narea. So, it remains to be seen yet if any forcing will be strong \nenough to break this cap. Something to monitor in the coming days.",
